WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:12.500
To live on Earth is to live as part 
of a network:

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.920
a giant web where almost every creature

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.280
depends on others for survival.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.200
These networks are so complex that it's
difficult to predict how resilient

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.200
they might be to changing conditions.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.960
Let's take an ecosystem in Australia,
where certain plants

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.540
have a symbiotic relationship with various ants.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.520
The ants act as bodyguards
protecting the plants

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.320
who in return provide food and shelter for the ants.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.040
But it's not like every kind of plant
has its own species of ant;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:46.720
that would be much too easy.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.620
In fact, each ant can visit 
a variety of plants

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.640
and each plant benefits from the protection

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.180
of a number of different
kinds of ant.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:59.380
You can see how quickly a network
becomes very complicated.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.940
Let's take all the plants in the
ecosystem and draw lines

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:08.740
between any two species that share the same ants.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.820
This ecosystem can be represented 
by a network.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.080
The plants are the nodes and the
connecting lines are the ants.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.360
Two plants are linked if they share 
at least one ant species.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.660
If they share more than one, 
the link is even stronger.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.900
By helping the ants, these plants are also
indirectly helping each other.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:32.920
The success of one plant
might lead to more ants

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.320
which could benefit another plant.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.060
Having lots of connections also
means the network can adapt to change.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.820
For example, along the bottom is a list
of all the ant species in the system.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:50.500
If one of these ants disappears 
the network can compensate.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.500
It's resilient...

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.360
...up to a point.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.300
As we remove ant species
at random, one by one,

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.940
the connections in the network grow 
weaker and start to disappear.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:07.320
As we go along, as well as losing 
whole plant species

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.080
the abundance of remaining 
species gradually decreases as well.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.880
There comes a key point 
for every network

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.520
where the population of the 
remaining species suddenly crashes.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.120
The network can no longer 
compensate for further changes.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:28.280
Where this point comes
represents the network's resilience.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:31.640
This resilience is usually thought to be
completely unpredictable

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:35.000
as it varies so much between 
different networks

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.180
but researchers have developed 
a new tool to try and simplify things.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.700
They started with complex,
multi-dimensional networks,

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.020
all with different properties.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.780
The clever part is
then using various mathematical techniques

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:53.600
to transform all these
networks into a standard model

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.040
in which the crash is always in the same place.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:02.020
This means that the resilience of the
original networks is now predictable.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:09.080
This can be useful in predicting how
ecosystems might react to environmental changes.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:12.420
Climate change, for example, can
affect all species in a network

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.000
and their relationships,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.520
weakening the strength
of all the connections.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.460
This can make the resilience of a
network so low

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.540
that it might only take the removal 
of a handful of species

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:24.560
to bring about complete collapse.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.160
This is particularly worrying for
already fragile ecosystems

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.560
in which a tiny change could cause the extinction

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.020
of a whole host of plants and animals.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.760
That's why it's important to have tools
to predict and prevent

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.978
these catastrophic network failures,

00:03:40.978 --> 00:03:43.380
like the disappearance 
of honey bees

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:46.360
or unexpected collapses in
commercial fisheries.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.300
And these models don't just 
apply to ecological systems.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:53.360
The same tools can be used 
to examine networks of people

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:55.780
from social networks to the
spread of epidemics,

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.320
banking networks in a financial crisis

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.220
and even networks of air traffic 
in which a single problem

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.900
could cause huge delays.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.680
Models of all these things 
are often available already,

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.220
based on specific real-world data,

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.600
but these new mathematical tools can be

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.980
applied generally to a whole range of
different systems.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:20.100
They should allow us to predict how any 
network will respond to change

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.480
and could even suggest specific strategies

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:25.460
for preventing catastrophic
damage.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.060
Whether its plants, planes or people

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.340
we may be a little closer to
understanding

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:35.780
the giant, complex networks of our planet.

